{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>Forsake, O simple ones</b> — a description of those characterized by simplicity. Its sense: forsake, O simple ones, and turn to me. Another interpretation: a gathering of the simple ones there, like: <i>תאהבו פתי</i> (you simple ones love), in the manner of <i>אל תהי מרי כבית המרי</i>. And it is possible further to interpret that it is the words of Wisdom addressed to the simple ones, saying to them: forsake your ways, O simple ones, and live; in the manner of <i>הרפו</i>; and know that I am Elohim." "<b>and him who walks</b> — a verb from the root כונן, in the Assyrian [form]." "<b>in the way of understanding</b> — and the way of understanding, it is wisdoms, and her house built upon seven pillars."], :book :proverbs, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 9, :notes {0 "The last clause echoes Psalms 46:10 (הרפו ודעו כי אנכי אלהים); the sense of כדרך הרפו is 'in the manner of [the verse] הרפו.'", 2 "'Assyrian [form]' (אשורי) is Ibn Ezra's term for a conjugation/verb pattern; the gloss is literal. The lemma אשרו corresponds to the verse's ויישרה; Ibn Ezra reads it as derived from כונן."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Proverbs -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/mishley/"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:21:44.529458194Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}